# Defect 031's rule at a `transfers` position (panel 145; panel 176's item 2).
# A transfer ends the caller's obligation as `consumes` does, so a borrowed
# value may not be handed to either, and the refusal is the same code. What
# differs is what it says the callee does: a transfer hands the life into
# another value that will end it, and until 2026-09-25 the message said the
# callee "ends the life of what it is given" here too, which is false of
# `h_put`. The repair in the note, `@` on both lines, is the same.
extern "ffi-transfers-a-borrowed-handle.h"
    record H tag hh
    function h_open() -> H acquires h_close
    function h_close(x: H consumes)
    function h_put(parent: H, child: H transfers h_close)

record Holder
    cell: H

# The refusal. `h` is borrowed, and `h_put` hands what it is given away.
function attached(parent: H, h: Holder)
    h_put(parent: parent, child: h.cell)  #~ consumed_borrowed_handle

# An `@` parameter says on the signature that the value does not survive.
function attached_well(parent: H, @h: Holder)
    h_put(parent: parent, child: h.cell)

function main()
    root = h_open()
    a: Holder @ Holder(cell: h_open())
    attached(parent: root, h: a)
    attached_well(parent: root, @a)
    h_close(x: root)
